        "output": "1\n4\n2\n2 3\n0\n1\n2",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: We perform a single operation choosing i=4i = 4i=4. This does the following:\n\nSet S3=f(S3,S4)=f(0,0)=1S_3 = f(S_3, S_4) = f(0, 0) = 1S3​=f(S3​,S4​)=f(0,0)=1.\nSet S5=f(S5,S4)=f(0,0)=1S_5 = f(S_5, S_4) = f(0, 0) = 1S5​=f(S5​,S4​)=f(0,0)=1.\n\nThe string is now 111011111011111011, and contains 555 ones.\nThis is the best we can do - it can be shown that attaining 666 ones is impossible.\nTest case 222: We perform two operations:\n\nChoose i=2i = 2i=2, which updates S1S_1S1​ and S3S_3S3​. The string is now 100011110001111000111.\nChoose i=3i = 3i=3, which updates S2S_2S2​ and S4S_4S4​. The string is now 110111111011111101111.\n\nThis is the best we can do.\nTest case 333: Every character is already 111, so nothing needs to be done.\nTest case 444: Choosing i=2i = 2i=2 (our only possible move) results in the string 001001001.\nThis has one 111, and is the best we can do.\nNote that the original string also had 111 one, but since we aren't aiming to minimize the number of operations, this output is accepted too."
